1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,573
Picture a small hotel on a Friday evening.

2
00:00:03,643 --> 00:00:10,385
Two guests walk up to the front desk, both holding a confirmation for room two-oh-four, for the same night.

3
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:13,463
Somewhere, a spreadsheet said the room was free twice.

4
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:17,033
That is the kind of mistake I wanted to make impossible.

5
00:00:17,483 --> 00:00:21,195
So I built StayDesk, a front-desk app for hotels.

6
00:00:21,265 --> 00:00:30,375
And then, for this capstone, I took it all the way from my laptop to a monitored, autoscaled Kubernetes cluster on AWS.

7
00:00:30,445 --> 00:00:33,625
Let me show you the app first, and then the journey.

8
00:00:34,875 --> 00:00:38,652
This is the dashboard the front desk opens every morning.

9
00:00:38,722 --> 00:00:46,424
At the top: occupancy, today's arrivals and departures, guests in the house, and the revenue booked this month.

10
00:00:46,874 --> 00:00:49,498
Below that is the worklist for today.

11
00:00:49,568 --> 00:00:52,022
Who is arriving, who is due to leave.

12
00:00:52,092 --> 00:00:59,837
On the right, every room in the hotel, coloured by its state: occupied, ready, waiting for housekeeping, or out of service.

13
00:01:01,087 --> 00:01:02,773
A guest calls to book.

14
00:01:02,843 --> 00:01:06,044
I enter her name and email and choose the dates.

15
00:01:06,114 --> 00:01:07,650
Notice the room list.

16
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:13,673
It only offers rooms that are actually free for those nights, and that are big enough for the party.

17
00:01:13,743 --> 00:01:18,245
The price is worked out as I go: nights, times the nightly rate.

18
00:01:18,695 --> 00:01:21,576
I confirm, and the booking appears straight away.

19
00:01:21,646 --> 00:01:30,670
Behind the scenes the API checks for overlaps again, so even two front-desk staff clicking at the same moment can't double-book a room.

20
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:33,990
Now an arrival walks in.

21
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,493
One click, and the guest is checked in.

22
00:01:36,563 --> 00:01:39,401
The room turns occupied on the dashboard.

23
00:01:39,851 --> 00:01:47,190
When a guest checks out, the room goes to "needs cleaning", and the system won't let anyone check into a dirty room.

24
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:52,487
Housekeeping marks it clean here, on the rooms page, and it is ready to sell again.

25
00:01:52,557 --> 00:01:57,017
Small rules, but they are exactly the ones a busy desk forgets.

26
00:01:58,267 --> 00:02:02,257
An app that only runs on my laptop is half a project.

27
00:02:02,327 --> 00:02:12,354
The real question for this course was: how does a change travel from my keyboard to real users, safely, and how do we know it is healthy once it is there?

28
00:02:12,804 --> 00:02:14,938
That journey has thirteen stops.

29
00:02:15,008 --> 00:02:22,689
On the top row: the application, git, the CI pipeline, the Docker image, the security scan and the registry.

30
00:02:22,759 --> 00:02:30,482
On the bottom row: Terraform, Kubernetes, Helm, Ingress, autoscaling, monitoring, and finally troubleshooting.

31
00:02:31,732 --> 00:02:34,293
Here are the numbers from the real run.

32
00:02:34,363 --> 00:02:36,603
Forty-nine automated tests.

33
00:02:36,673 --> 00:02:40,066
Zero fixable high or critical vulnerabilities.

34
00:02:40,136 --> 00:02:47,091
Three minutes and twenty-one seconds from a git push to the new version running on Amazon EKS.

35
00:02:47,161 --> 00:02:52,879
Sixty-one AWS resources created by Terraform, and then destroyed.

36
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:56,875
The backend scaling itself from two pods to six under load.

37
00:02:56,945 --> 00:03:00,082
All of it for about forty cents of cloud credits.

38
00:03:01,332 --> 00:03:02,911
It starts with git.

39
00:03:02,981 --> 00:03:10,747
Every change is a small commit with a clear message: a feature, a fix, a test, the pipeline, the infrastructure.

40
00:03:10,817 --> 00:03:12,674
Secrets never go in.

41
00:03:12,744 --> 00:03:20,361
Environment files, Terraform state and variable files are ignored, and I scanned the whole history to be sure.

42
00:03:21,611 --> 00:03:24,705
Every push to main wakes up GitHub Actions.

43
00:03:24,775 --> 00:03:32,029
First the tests: linting, and those forty-nine pytest cases, each on its own throwaway database.

44
00:03:32,099 --> 00:03:35,364
If a single test fails, nothing after it runs.

45
00:03:35,434 --> 00:03:37,738
Bad code never becomes an image.

46
00:03:38,188 --> 00:03:43,992
Then both images are built and tagged with the commit hash, scanned, pushed, and deployed.

47
00:03:44,062 --> 00:03:49,609
One detail I care about: every third-party action is pinned to an exact commit.

48
00:03:49,679 --> 00:03:55,504
In March this year, attackers hijacked the version tags of a popular security action.

49
00:03:55,574 --> 00:03:57,516
A pinned hash can't be moved.

50
00:03:58,766 --> 00:04:03,311
The backend and the frontend each get a multi-stage Docker image.

51
00:04:03,381 --> 00:04:05,621
Build tools stay in the build stage.

52
00:04:05,691 --> 00:04:12,711
What ships is small: the frontend runtime is twenty-six megabytes, with no Node.js inside.

53
00:04:12,781 --> 00:04:15,235
And neither container runs as root.

54
00:04:15,305 --> 00:04:23,305
With one command, docker compose up, the whole stack starts on any laptop: the database, the API, and the website.

55
00:04:24,555 --> 00:04:32,876
Before an image is allowed into the registry, Trivy scans it: the operating system packages and every Python library.

56
00:04:32,946 --> 00:04:34,504
The rule is strict.

57
00:04:34,574 --> 00:04:40,228
Any high or critical vulnerability that already has a fix, and the pipeline stops.

58
00:04:40,298 --> 00:04:41,920
The result here is zero.

59
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:44,568
That doesn't mean "secure forever".

60
00:04:44,638 --> 00:04:50,953
New vulnerabilities appear every day, which is exactly why every single build is scanned again.

61
00:04:52,203 --> 00:04:56,022
Images that pass go to the GitHub Container Registry.

62
00:04:56,092 --> 00:04:58,034
There is no "latest" tag.

63
00:04:58,104 --> 00:05:01,646
Each image is named after the commit it was built from.

64
00:05:01,716 --> 00:05:03,616
Commit A becomes image A.

65
00:05:03,686 --> 00:05:09,617
So if anything misbehaves in production, I can point to the exact line of code that is running.

66
00:05:10,867 --> 00:05:12,297
Next, the cloud.

67
00:05:12,367 --> 00:05:16,570
I didn't click through the AWS console to build anything.

68
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:37,697
Terraform describes the whole platform as code: a network across two availability zones, public and private subnets, a NAT gateway, an EKS cluster on Kubernetes one point three five, a worker node, and a role that lets GitHub deploy without storing a single AWS password.

69
00:05:38,147 --> 00:05:43,545
The plan said sixty-one resources to add, and Terraform built every one of them.

70
00:05:44,795 --> 00:05:50,364
And here they are, in the real AWS console: the cluster, active and healthy.

71
00:05:50,434 --> 00:05:51,608
The network.

72
00:05:51,678 --> 00:05:53,193
The four subnets.

73
00:05:53,263 --> 00:05:54,693
The NAT gateway.

74
00:05:54,763 --> 00:05:57,900
And the worker node, passing all its health checks.

75
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,796
Now the pipeline can ship to the cluster.

76
00:06:01,866 --> 00:06:05,600
GitHub hands the job a short-lived identity token.

77
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:11,601
AWS checks that it really comes from my repository's main branch, and lets it in.

78
00:06:11,671 --> 00:06:16,237
The job runs helm upgrade, waits for the rollout, and posts a summary.

79
00:06:16,687 --> 00:06:23,707
The result: two backend pods, two frontend pods, and a PostgreSQL database on its own disk.

80
00:06:23,777 --> 00:06:26,039
All running, with zero restarts.

81
00:06:27,289 --> 00:06:30,127
All of that is packaged as one Helm chart.

82
00:06:30,197 --> 00:06:39,030
Deployments, services, the database, secrets, the ingress, the autoscaler, network rules and monitoring, in one versioned package.

83
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:43,815
The same chart deploys to a local test cluster and to EKS.

84
00:06:43,885 --> 00:06:45,742
Only the values change.

85
00:06:45,812 --> 00:06:49,396
And if a release goes wrong, one command rolls it back.

86
00:06:50,646 --> 00:06:52,375
How do users reach it?

87
00:06:52,445 --> 00:06:53,875
Through one address.

88
00:06:53,945 --> 00:06:59,727
Traffic comes in through an AWS load balancer to Traefik, the ingress controller.

89
00:06:59,797 --> 00:07:02,464
Requests for the website go to the frontend.

90
00:07:02,534 --> 00:07:05,799
Anything under slash API goes to the backend.

91
00:07:06,249 --> 00:07:18,047
I chose Traefik on purpose: the old community ingress-nginx project was retired this March, and I didn't want to build on something that no longer gets security fixes.

92
00:07:19,297 --> 00:07:21,687
Then I tried to break it with traffic.

93
00:07:21,757 --> 00:07:24,787
Four load generators hammered the API.

94
00:07:24,857 --> 00:07:34,500
CPU shot to over three hundred percent of what each pod had asked for, and within a minute the autoscaler had gone from two backend pods to six.

95
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:36,978
But here is the honest part.

96
00:07:37,048 --> 00:07:39,160
The sixth pod stayed pending.

97
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:45,268
To save money I was running a single node, and the autoscaler adds pods, not machines.

98
00:07:45,338 --> 00:07:48,880
In production, a cluster autoscaler would add the node.

99
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:51,255
I'd rather show you that than hide it.

100
00:07:52,505 --> 00:08:08,186
While all that was happening, Prometheus was scraping every pod every fifteen seconds, and Grafana turned it into this dashboard: requests per second, latency, error rate, and the autoscaler's replicas climbing in real time.

101
00:08:08,256 --> 00:08:17,088
One hundred and sixty-eight requests a second, ninety-five milliseconds at the ninety-fifth percentile, and zero server errors.

102
00:08:18,338 --> 00:08:19,725
Things will break.

103
00:08:19,795 --> 00:08:23,103
So I built three failures I can trigger on purpose.

104
00:08:23,173 --> 00:08:25,477
An image tag that doesn't exist.

105
00:08:25,547 --> 00:08:28,577
A service whose label selector matches nothing.

106
00:08:28,647 --> 00:08:33,704
And a pod that is running, but never ready, because it can't reach its database.

107
00:08:33,774 --> 00:08:38,916
For each one: the symptom, the command that reveals it, the root cause, and the fix.

108
00:08:40,166 --> 00:08:42,919
And then there were the failures I didn't plan.

109
00:08:42,989 --> 00:08:51,267
On a brand-new AWS account, the worker nodes never appeared: the free plan only allows certain instance types.

110
00:08:51,337 --> 00:08:56,457
The load balancer sat at pending: new accounts can't create the newer kinds yet.

111
00:08:56,527 --> 00:09:02,800
And the deploy was rejected because GitHub had changed the format of its identity tokens.

112
00:09:02,870 --> 00:09:09,228
Every one of those was found by reading the logs, fixed in code, and committed with the reasoning.

113
00:09:10,478 --> 00:09:12,143
So that is StayDesk.

114
00:09:12,213 --> 00:09:23,648
A real workflow for a hotel front desk, and a path from a single commit to a tested, scanned, deployed, autoscaled and monitored application on AWS.

115
00:09:23,718 --> 00:09:27,068
Then torn down cleanly, for about forty cents.

116
00:09:27,518 --> 00:09:32,447
Everything you saw is in the repository, with the evidence for every step.

117
00:09:32,517 --> 00:09:34,160
Thank you for watching.

